## Hypotheses
H1 directives didn't apply  -> DISPROVEN (R2 + R4 directive effect 1.19x is real)
H2 gcc flags didn't apply    -> DISPROVEN (R3 AVX present + R4 gcc effect 1.05x is real)
H3 measurement/harness wrong -> DISPROVEN (csr CONTROL 1.964x on identical rig; IQR<2%;
   pairing verified setup_sha csr 32d12490 / pava 3d2b1484; thermal 0 throttle)
H4 genuine kernel property   -> CONFIRMED

## Root cause
The PAVA reference kernel (numeric-loop path, §0.5.1b) has a SEQUENTIAL, division-
bound pooling loop (pool-adjacent-violators). It does not vectorize (packed-double=0)
and the bounds-check overhead is modest relative to the per-block divisions, so the
known-good config gains only ~1.25x — below the §0.3 numeric-loop threshold of 1.5x.
The INSTRUMENT is PROVEN CORRECT: csr_scale (raw-pointer) gets 1.964x on the SAME rig
and build pipeline (its inner data[j]*=f vectorizes AND is bounds-check-bound), the
two build arms are verified distinct, and the 2x2 decomposition responds sensibly.

## Disposition
Not an instrument bug (proven) and NOT a code defect -> no D-log, no post-mortem (which
requires a validated fix). The I-1 numeric-loop threshold (1.5x, §0.3) and the choice of
PAVA as the numeric-loop reference (§0.5.1b) are roadmap-PINNED. Resolving the gate
requires a roadmap-level decision (swap the numeric-loop reference kernel for one whose
loop genuinely clears 1.5x, OR re-examine the threshold). Per CLAUDE.md I must NOT change
a pinned kernel/threshold to force a pass, and must ask the human on roadmap ambiguity.
=> ESCALATE to the human. Raw + gate + diagnostic committed as the honest red gate.

